'''wis<sup>3</sup>†''', ''v.'' A spurious word, arising from a misunderstanding of the Middle English adverb ''iwis'', often written ''i-wis'', and in Middle English manuscripts ''i wis'', ''I wis'', whence it has been taken as the pronoun ''I'' with a verb ''wis'', vaguely regarded as connected with ''wit'' (which has a preterit ''wist''). See ''iwis'', and, for the real verb, see ''wit''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|Which book, advisedly read, and diligently followed but one year at home in England, would do a young gentleman more good, I ''wiss'', than three years’ travell abroad.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 65.}}}}
{{smaller block|Where my morning haunts are he ''wisses'' not.
{{right|''Milton'', Apology for Smectymnuus.}}}}

'''wisard†''', ''n.'' and ''a.'' An obsolete spelling of ''wizard''.

