¶
{{smaller block|Moses was learned in all the ''wisdom'' of the Egyptians.
{{right|Acts vii. 22.}}}}
{{smaller block|The Doctors laden with so many badges or cognisances of ''wisdom.''
{{right|''Foxe'' (Arber’s Eng. Garner, I. 105).}}}}
'''3.''' With possessive pronouns used as a personification (like “your highness,” etc.).
{{smaller block|''Viola.'' I saw thee late at the Count Orsino’s.<br>''Clown.'' . . . I think I saw ''your wisdom'' there.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iii. 1. 47.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Do, my good fools, my honest pious coxcombs,
My wary fools too! have I caught ''your wisdoms?''</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wife for a Month, iv. 1.}}}}
'''4.''' A wise saying or act; a wise thing.
{{smaller block|They which do eate or drinke, hauyng those ''wisdomes'' euer in sighte, . . . may sussitate some disputation or reasonynge wherby some part of tyme shall be saued whiche els . . . wolde be idely consumed.
{{right|''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, ii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|One of her many ''wisdoms.'' ''Mrs. H. Jackson'', Ramona, i.}}
'''5.''' Skill; skilfulness.
{{smaller block|And I have filled him with the spirit of God, in ''wisdom'', and in understanding, and in knowledge, and in all manner of workmanship.
{{right|Ex. xxxi. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|[In Scripture the word is sometimes specifically used, especially in Paul’s Epistles, in an opprobrious sense to designate the theosophical speculations (1 Cor. i. 19, 20) or rhetorical arts (1 Cor. ii. 5) current among the Greeks and Romans in the first century; sometimes in a good sense to designate spiritual perception of, accompanied with obedience to, the divine law (Prov. iii. 13; Acts vi. 3). Sometimes (as in Prov. viii.) it has personal attributes assigned to it.]}}
