'''wisdom-tooth''' (wiz′dọm-töth), ''n.'' The last molar tooth on either side of each jaw. {{smaller|It appears ordinarily between the ages of 20 and 25, presumably years of discretion (whence the name). Also technically called ''dens sapientiæ''. Also ''wit-tooth''.}}
{{smaller block|It seems to me in these days they’re all born with their ''wisdom-teeth'' cut and their whiskers growed.
{{right|''Whyte Melville'', White Rose, II. xxvi.}}}}

'''wise'''<sup>1</sup> (wīz), ''a.'' [< ME. ''wis'', ''wys'', < AS. ''wīs'' = OS. OFries. ''wīs'' = D. ''wijs'' = MLG. ''wīs'', LG. ''wis'' = OHG. ''wīs'', ''wīsi'', MHG. ''wīs'', ''wīse'', G. ''weise'' = Icel. ''vīss'' = Sw. Dan. ''vis'' = Goth. ''weis'' (in comp. ''unweis'', unwise), wise; prob. orig. *''wītsa'', *''wītta'', with pp. formative, from the root of AS. ''witan'', etc., E. ''wit''<sup>1</sup>, know: see ''wit''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Having the power of discerning and judging rightly, or of discriminating between what is true and what is false, between that which is right, fit, and proper and that which is unsuitable, injudicious, and wrong; possessed of discernment, discretion, and judgment: as, a ''wise'' prince; a ''wise'' magistrate.
{{smaller block|Five of them were ''wise'', and five were foolish.
{{right|Mat. xxv. 2.}}
<poem>We, ignorant of ourselves,
Beg often our own harms, which the ''wise'' powers
Deny us for our good.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., ii. 1. 6.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>A ''wise'' man
Accepts all fair occasions of advancement;
Flies no commodity for fear of danger,
Ventures and gains, lives easily, drinks good wine,
Fares neatly, is richly cloath’d, in worthiest company.</poem>
{{right|''T. Tomkis'' (?), Albumazar, ii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|I am foolish old Mayberry, and yet I can be ''wise'' Mayberry, too. {{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|You read of but one ''wise'' Man, and all that he knew was, that he knew nothing. {{right|''Congreve'', Old Bachelor, i. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' Proper to a wise man; sage; grave; serious.
{{smaller block|<poem>One rising, eminent,
In ''wise'' deport, spake much of right and wrong.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xi. 666.}}}}
'''3.''' Having knowledge; knowing; intelligent; enlightened; learned; erudite.
{{smaller block|<poem>Bote ther were fewe men so ''wys'' that couthe the wei thider,
Bote bustelyng forth as bestes ouer valeyes and hulles,
For while thei wente here owen wille thei wente alle amys.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), vi. 4.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thou shalbe ''wisest'' of wit,—this wete thou for sothe,—
And know all the conyng that kyndly is for men.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2411.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Where ignorance is bliss,
’Tis folly to be ''wise.''</poem>
{{right|''Gray'', On a Distant Prospect of Eton College.}}}}
